# Nuclear Chemistry and Chemistry in Everyday Life

> The nucleus can shed particles or split outright, releasing a
> million times the energy of chemical bonds. Add medicines,
> additives, and fertilizers, and chemistry explains your whole day.

## 1. The Discovery of Radioactivity

[definition]
**Radioactivity** is the spontaneous decay of unstable nuclei,
emitting radiation without any external trigger. Henri Becquerel
discovered it in 1896 — uranium salts fogged photographic plates in
the dark — and Marie and Pierre Curie went on to isolate polonium
and radium.
[/definition]

## 2. Alpha, Beta, Gamma

| Property | Alpha (α) | Beta (β) | Gamma (γ) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Identity | helium nucleus ⁴₂He | electron ⁰₋₁e | high-energy photon |
| Charge | +2 | −1 | 0 |
| Mass | 4 u | ~1/1836 u | 0 |
| Speed | ~5–7% of c | up to 98% of c | c (light speed) |
| Stopped by | paper or skin | a few mm of aluminium | thick lead or concrete |
| Ionizing power | highest | medium | lowest |
| Field deflection | toward negative plate | toward positive plate | none |

@diagram tree id="decay-modes" layout="tree" aria="Radioactive decay modes: alpha, beta and gamma with their effects on mass number and atomic number"
  "Radioactive decay" -> "Alpha: A - 4, Z - 2"
  "Radioactive decay" -> "Beta: A same, Z + 1"
  "Radioactive decay" -> "Gamma: A, Z unchanged"
@/diagram

[example]
**Beta decay of carbon-14:** ¹⁴C → ¹⁴N + ⁰₋₁e. A neutron becomes a
proton, so Z rises from 6 to 7 while A stays 14.
[/example]

## 3. Half-Life

[definition]
The **half-life** (t½) is the time for half the nuclei in a sample
to decay. Carbon-14's half-life of 5,730 years powers radiocarbon
dating; iodine-131's 8-day half-life suits short medical uses.
[/definition]

| Half-lives elapsed | 1 | 2 | 3 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Fraction remaining | 1/2 | 1/4 | 1/8 |

## 4. Fission and Fusion

| | Fission | Fusion |
| --- | --- | --- |
| What happens | heavy nucleus splits | light nuclei merge |
| Fuel | U-235, Pu-239 | hydrogen isotopes |
| Where | reactors, atomic bombs | the Sun and stars, H-bombs |
| Example | n + ²³⁵U → ¹⁴¹Ba + ⁹²Kr + 3n + energy | 4 ¹H → ⁴He + energy |

Einstein's mass–energy equivalence explains the enormous output:

@math label="eq:emc2"
E = mc^{2}
@/math

Reactors use **moderators** (water, heavy water, graphite) to slow
neutrons and **control rods** (cadmium, boron) to absorb them,
keeping the **chain reaction** steady. Fusion demands the Sun's
temperatures; the ITER project aims to tame it on Earth.

## 5. Applications and Hazards

| Application | Isotope | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Cancer radiotherapy | Co-60 | gamma beams target tumours |
| Thyroid therapy | I-131 | the thyroid hoards iodine |
| Organ imaging | Tc-99m | the workhorse of hospital scans |
| Radiocarbon dating | C-14 | dates one-living matter to ~50,000 years |
| Food irradiation | Co-60 | delays sprouting, kills microbes |
| Smoke detectors | Am-241 | α-particles ionize air; smoke cuts the current |

[warning]
Radiation can damage DNA — causing radiation sickness, cancer, or
mutations. Shield with lead, maximize distance, minimize exposure
time, and test basement air for **radon**, the radioactive gas that
seeps from soil into homes.
[/warning]

## 6. Chemistry in the Medicine Cabinet

| Drug class | Examples | Job |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Antacids | milk of magnesia, ranitidine | neutralize or damp stomach acid |
| Antihistamines | cetirizine, promethazine | allergy relief |
| Analgesics | paracetamol, aspirin | pain and fever; aspirin thins blood |
| Antibiotics | penicillin, ofloxacin | kill or slow bacteria |

## 7. Additives, Cleansers, and Home Chemistry

| Category | Examples | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Preservatives | sodium benzoate, SO₂ | slow spoilage |
| Sweeteners | aspartame, sucralose | sweetness without the sugar load |
| Antioxidants | BHA, BHT | stop fats going rancid |
| Fertilizers | urea (N), superphosphate (P), potash (K) | NPK: growth, roots, disease resistance |

**Cleansing agents:** soaps biodegrade but fail in hard water;
synthetic detergents work in hard water — linear-chain versions
biodegrade, while branched ones persist in rivers.

**Fire extinguishers:**

| Type | Best on | Never on |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Water | wood, paper | electrical or oil fires |
| CO₂ | electrical, oil | — |
| Foam | petrol, oil | — |
| Dry powder | most fires | leaves residue |

## 8. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Most penetrating radiation? | gamma |
| Alpha particle is? | a helium nucleus, charge +2 |
| Beta decay changes? | Z + 1, A unchanged |
| C-14 half-life? | 5,730 years |
| Sun's power source? | fusion of hydrogen |
| Smoke detector isotope? | Am-241 |
